      "title": "In 1984, an anonymous poster said a Pink Floyd album hid a prize. Nobody ever found it",
      "content_html": "<a href=\"https://boingboing.net/2026/08/01/publius-enigma.html\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"In 1984, an anonymous poster said a Pink Floyd album hid a prize. Nobody ever found it\"><img alt=\"Publius Enigma — Lagw62 / CC BY-SA 4.0 (Wikimedia Commons)\" class=\"webfeedsFeaturedVisual wp-post-image\" height=\"849\" src=\"https://i0.wp.com/boingboing.net/wp-content/uploads/2026/08/1280px-Facade_of_Earls_Court2C_London2C_for_Pink_FloydE28099s_Division_Bell_tour.jpg?fit=1280%2C849&amp;quality=60&amp;ssl=1\" style=\"display: block; margin: auto; margin-bottom: 5px;\" width=\"1280\" /></a><p class=\"wp-block-paragraph\">On June 11, 1994, a user posting anonymously through a remailer to the Pink Floyd newsgroup on Usenet told fans that <a href=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Publius_Enigma\">the band's new album</a> had \"a central purpose and a designed solution\" buried in it. Study the artwork, the lyrics, and the music, the poster said. &#8212; <a class=\"read-more\" href=\"https://boingboing.net/2026/08/01/publius-enigma.html\">Read the rest </a></p>\n<p>The post <a href=\"https://boingboing.net/2026/08/01/publius-enigma.html\">In 1984, an anonymous poster said a Pink Floyd album hid a prize. Nobody ever found it</a> appeared first on <a href=\"https://boingboing.net\">Boing Boing</a>.</p>",
